    "lawCode": "Rpld §§13 - 19, §813, §806 sub 3, Art 12, amd §§808, 810, 811 & 812, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5647\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the general municipal law, in relation\nto making technical corrections thereto; and to repeal certain\nprovisions of such law relating thereto\n\nPURPOSE:  The purpose of this bill is to repeal certain provisions of\nthe general municipal law in order to streamline the statute and make\ngovernment more efficient.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:  This bill repeals sections 13, 14, 15, 16, 17,\n18, 19 and 813; article 12; and subdivision 3 of section 806 of the\ngeneral municipal law.  The bill amends subdivision 5 of section 808,\nparagraph (a) of subdivision 1 and, paragraph (a) of subdivision 3 of\nsection 812 of the general municipal law, as added by chapter 813 of\nthe laws of 1987.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  This bill is the first change in law emerging from a\nnew ongoing legislative initiative that is specifically aimed at\nmaking government more efficient. The New York State Assembly\nCommittee on Oversight, Analysis and Investigation, in coordination\nwith other standing committees, is undertaking a comprehensive review\nof tens of thousands of reporting requirements currently written into\nevery area of New York State Law.\n\nThe purpose of this extensive review is to enhance government\nefficiency. Our staff is actively looking to eliminate unnecessary\nreporting requirements and, by extension, some other statutorily\nrequired activities by various agencies, municipalities, and other\npolitical subdivisions that no longer serve the purpose for which they\nwere originally placed into statute. After the first several months of\nthis review, it has become clear that many of these requirements are\noutdated, duplicative, or unused and therefore no longer necessary.\nThis bill is the first in what is projected to be a very long series\nof repeal bills that address these superfluous sections of law.\n\nSpecifically, this bill repeals reporting requirements and several\nother provisions pertaining to The Board of Railroad Commissioners and\nits related proceedings. The initial enabling legislation and the\noverall practices of this board are outdated, having been employed at\nthe end of the 19th Century. Second, the bill repeals the provisions\nof the general municipal law with regard to the temporary state\ncommission on local government ethics and its reporting requirements.\nThis is necessary because the commission sunset in 1993; however,\nlegal references and cross-references have left these reporting\nrequirements scattered throughout the general municipal law, resulting\nin various agencies and commission having to still abide by these\nrequirements.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: This is a new bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  This act shall take effect on immediately.\n\n",
